**1. Analysis of the Attached PDF**

The attached PDFs describe Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle derived from sweet corn phytoglycogen, as an effective vaccine adjuvant. Nano-11, measuring 70-80 nm with a positive surface charge, efficiently adsorbs negatively charged antigens. *In vitro*, it enhances antigen uptake and activation of dendritic cells (DCs), increasing costimulatory molecule expression and IL-1β secretion. *In vivo*, Nano-11 significantly boosts antigen-specific antibody responses, comparable to or exceeding aluminum adjuvants. It induces a transient, localized inflammatory response primarily involving monocytes/macrophages, targets antigens to migratory DCs in draining lymph nodes, and clears gradually from the injection site without systemic distribution.

**2. Independent Analysis of the Image**

This image comprises eight immunofluorescence micrographs of tissue sections, likely skeletal muscle, presented in a 2x4 grid. Each panel shows cellular structures stained blue (nuclei). The top row additionally displays numerous green-stained cells (suggesting immune cells like monocytes/macrophages) and red-stained particulate material, often appearing internalized by the green cells. The bottom row, in contrast, shows very few green-stained cells or red particulate material, primarily displaying muscle tissue with blue nuclei. The images appear to compare two conditions, one with significant cellular infiltration and particle presence (top) versus another with minimal activity (bottom).

**3. Relevance of the Image to the Scientific Topic of the PDF**

The image is highly relevant to the strictly scientific topic of the PDF. It is Figure 10 from the 2015 Journal of Controlled Release paper, illustrating Nano-11's *in vivo* effects. The top row, representing tissue injected with AF647-labeled Nano-11 (red) and OVA, vividly demonstrates the induced inflammatory response (green-stained Mac-2+ monocytes/macrophages and MHCII+ cells) and their efficient uptake of the Nano-11 nanoparticles. The bottom row, showing soluble OVA only, serves as a control, depicting minimal cellular infiltration. This directly supports the PDF's findings on Nano-11 inducing local inflammation and enhancing antigen delivery to immune cells *in vivo*.